[Music]  let's all take a journey back in time to  that first  curious fish who crawled out of the  ocean onto that warm sandy beach  about 400 million years ago first off  what a crazy experience that must have  been for the fish  i imagine they're turning around looking  lost being like  hey uh do you guys know there's a whole  world out here  so that first fish was essentially the  iphone 1 of land animals  it was a prototype a minimum viable  product  it was really exciting but definitely  not perfect  but each generation that fishes kids got  better at surviving  bigger lungs and feet and pretty soon  they were running instead of swimming  in essence they iterated through  evolution  so why does that sound so familiar at a  business school  it's because evolution is just finding  product market fit  let me say that again evolution is just  finding product market fit  now we have everything from beavers to  butterflies each one unique and  incredibly well adapted  to what it does okay yes i know  butterflies flutter around a little bit  but  i don't see you with a drone that can  run on nectar  so how can we draw insights from nature  to design our own technology the answer  is biomimicry biomimicry  is nature inspired innovation and it is  incredibly overlooked opportunity for  humanity my name is benjamin cramer  and i'm an mba student here at the gsb  and today  we're going to talk about how biomimicry  could be the key to unlocking some of  society's greatest technical challenges  so first let's start with a couple  examples so you can see what i'm talking  about  there's a crisp autumn morning in the  1940s and a french man  was out walking his dog the same path  they walked every day  and his dog took off into the bushes  when it came back a couple minutes later  wagging his tail and very proud of  himself  the man noticed that there was a couple  birds stuck in his dog's fur  and as he knelt over pulling each one  out one by one  the craziest thing happened he had an  epiphany  what if the little hooks holding these  seeds to my dog's fur  could be used to hold fabric together  without that insight we wouldn't have  velcro  and we'd be stuck tying shoes for every  five-year-old in the country  i had my own velcro moment about 10  years ago  i was hiking with my dad and brother in  zion national park  it was a warm summer's day one of those  that feels timeless  when i look down to see a little spider  hanging from a thread on my arm  and i thought to myself could we milk  spiders for thread my dad  ever the pragmatist pointed out that  milking spiders did not seem like the  best business model  and he was totally right so then i  thought  can we mimic it instead can we make  artificial spider silk  back home i rushed to my computer eager  to trademark the name silk with a y  instead of an i  of course and was super disappointed to  find out  that in fact someone was already doing  it after the requisite time for  teenage sulking i picked myself up and  kept searching for my next velcro moment  as an aside that company was bolt  threads and they've already raised 200  million dollars  if i could come up with that idea as a  teenager what could society accomplish  as a whole  if we all just looked a little deeper at  nature what could we build  if we look to the designs of the plants  and animals around us for inspiration  now some of you are probably thinking  okay yes  spider silk and velcro are great but is  anyone else really doing this  so let's go through a couple more  examples so i can really give you a  taste of the potential  for the 2000 summer olympics in sydney  speedo unveiled a new bathing suit  modeled after  a sharkskin and 80 of the swimmers that  year wearing speedo's suit  meddled on the totally other side of the  spectrum  neural networks being developed by  cutting edge ai firms are in part being  designed after the neural networks in  our own brains  and the best part is it can be applied  to any industry you just have to know  where to look  building a swarm of drones look at how a  flock of birds can fly at 35 miles an  hour without crashing  or an architectural firm looking to  design a green building  can look at how a termite mound is able  to keep the same temperature  all year round without any access to  electricity  we've discovered 1.2 million species on  earth and some people think there's as  many as 8.7 million  think of all the potential for society  if we just look to the designs of the  plants and animals around us  but we humans are a pretty cocky bunch  build a couple skyscrapers invent ice  cream  and we're pretty sure of ourselves  we think we can just sit back and come  up with new ideas out of thin air  okay not us but like some humans  probably  einstein once famously said you cannot  solve problems by using the same  thinking  that you used when you created them and  we're all too familiar  with the problems that our current  thinking has created  there's climate change resource  depletion  pollution i'm here to propose that  biomimicry can help us think  differently biomimicry can help us break  into entirely new areas of innovation  we don't need to rely on our own  ingenuity anymore to solve these  problems  there's a million guides out there just  waiting to help us out  20 years ago human-centered design was  introduced on the ted stage  today it's expected knowledge in most  companies and major business schools  around the world  so now it's time for nature  nature-centered design we need every  advantage in our fight to save our  planet and our health  and biomimicry could be what tips the  odds in our favor  so what do you see that nature has that  we should be copying  what can you invent to help push  innovation further  what can you take home from a walk in  the woods what is your  velcro moment

